     /Pg 1816 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

1829 0 obj
  << /ActualText (\133A2\135 1\0563\072 Q1\072 What is the main goal or objective of this level segment\077\n\nThere is a green circle pad on the top after the vertical segment which is used in the default side scroller to signify the end\054 this is also used in my example\056\n\n\133A2\135 1\0563\072 Q2\072 How does the level guide the player towards the objective\077\n\nThe player is funneled toward the final object which is a green pad on the top\056 The only other paths are dead ends\056\n\n\133A2\135 1\0563\072 Q3\072 How is the pacing and difficulty changed or introduced\077\n\nThe pacing is introduced very slowly as there is no real threat\056\n\n\133A2\135 1\0563\072 Q4\072 Where are the risk and reward elements in the level\077\n\nIf we had enemies there would be risks and rewards of entering and fighting the enemies in each room\054 \050a door is signified by the vertical block on the roof\051\056\n\n\133A2\135 1\0563\072 Q5\072 What are the unique mechanics or features in this level segment\077\n\nThe level is centered around the platforming aspect of the game\054 one more interesting segment is the vertical shaft to the exit\056)
     /K [ 13 ]
     /P 1830 0 R
     /Pg 1816 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

1830 0 obj
  << /K [ 12 1829 0 R ]
     /P 1997 0 R
     /S /P
     /Pg 1816 0 R
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

1831 0 obj
  << /ActualText (\133A2\135 3\0561\072 Q1\072 What was the core experience you wanted for the player\077\n\nMy intent was for the player to get a feel for the basic mechanics regarding platforming\056\n\n\133A2\135 3\0561\072 Q2\072 How does the layout support the goal of the level\077\n\nI tried to give lots of room for the player to experiment\054 which led to a fairly open level\056\n\n\133A2\135 3\0561\072 Q3\072 How did you balance difficulty and fairness\077\n\nBalance and fairness was pretty easy to deal with as there was not really any imminent threats\054 so players may keep trying until they get it\056\n\n\133A2\135 3\0561\072 Q4\072 How did you use risk and reward in your design\077\n\nMost of the risk and reward comes from the jumps themselves\054 as we were unable to create enemies you have to imagine the added pressure of an archer lining up their shot whilst the player is trying to platform away\056\n\n\133A2\135 3\0561\072 Q5\072 How does the final result compare to your initial vision\077\n\nWhile it wasn\031t exactly what I would have done if I had the extra room\054 within the limits of the assignment I am happy with it\056)
     /K [ 15 ]
     /P 1832 0 R
     /Pg 1816 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

