1837 0 obj
  << /ActualText (What is the movement concept\077 How should it feel to be the character\077 How would you describe their movement\077\n\nThe focus of the movement concept that I tried to imitate is a combat\055platformer game\056 Which means precise and combat should feel fairly lethal\054 granted due to a prototype we can\031t have combat so I just focused on the movement\056 Essentially I wanted to minimize the floatiness and make the character\031s movement feel snappy but also weighted at the same time\056 This would feel like near instant acceleration\054 and near instant deceleration\056\n\nWhat are some games that you can reference that give you a good sense of this\077\n\nWell Dead Cells was my primary reference\054 but I also drew on my knowledge of how games such as hollow knight feel\054 as it\031s also a combat\055platformer\056\n\nWhat do those games to to address Q1\077 What do they not do well\077\n\nDead Cells uses high gravity and scales the jump velocity of the player accordingly to minimize time in the air\054 they also use Coyote time and jump buffering to manipulate the perception of the player\056\n\nHow do your settings achieve this goal\056\050\nI focused on 3 areas\073 The player\031s jump\054 air control\054 and the acceleration\056 By changing the jump velocity and acceleration I could create a more punchy launch and increase the effect of gravity to make platforming faster\056\n\nHow did you strike a balance between responsive and jittery\077\n\nI did this by ensuring that the ground friction was fairly high whilst also tuning the braking force\054 my goal was to completely remove the slide feeling that the default unreal character had\056\n)
     /K [ 21 ]
     /P 1838 0 R
     /Pg 1816 0 R
     /S /Span
     /Type /StructElem
  >>
endobj

