1915 0 obj
  << /ActualText (Why did you choose this example\077 What makes it an effective reference for Moontower\077\nOne of Moontower\031s design philosophies is that of moving upwards to escape a place\056 This section in Dead Cells\031 highlights how you can utilize simple platforming to convey a sense of vertical progression\056 This would be a perfect between encounter transition section for our game\056 We can give the player a break from combat\054 whilst giving them a fun but short platforming section that conveys the vertical progression we\031re going for\056\n\nWhat player skills does this level test\077 Is it at a beginner\054 intermediate or advanced level of difficulty\077\nThis is a really simple section that tests the player\031s platforming\056 Its short but effective\056 I\031d rate it as beginner difficulty\056\n\nDo the aesthetic elements support the overall level design\077 Why or why not\077 \050Include specific examples to back up your argument\051\056\nThis level section reinforces the use of colors to differentiate the background from the foreground\054 creating a clear sense of where to go\056 This supports the level design well because it removes confusion from the player\056 I may be repeating myself but its for good reason\054 this design concept is crucial if we want players to progress fluidly through our game\056\n)
     /K [ 99 ]
     /P 1916 0 R
     /Pg 1816 0 R
     /S /Span
